---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
import { PAGE_HERO_IMAGES } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Medicolegal Fundamentals | Radiology & Medicolegal Law"
    description="The core principles of UK clinical negligence law applied to radiology: duty, breach, causation, consent and expert evidence."
>
    <PageHero
        eyebrow="Medicolegal fundamentals"
        title="Medicolegal Fundamentals"
        description="The legal principles that govern radiological practice and clinical negligence."
        heroImage={PAGE_HERO_IMAGES["/medicolegal-fundamentals"] || undefined}
    />

    <PageIntro
        eyebrow="Core legal framework"
        paragraphs={[
            'Most radiology claims turn on four questions: was a duty owed, did the radiologist fall below the required standard, did that breach cause harm, and was the patient properly informed where consent was required?',
            'This page covers the foundational principles only. Detailed legislation, professional standards and recent radiology cases are considered separately.'
        ]}
        contents={[
            { href: '#framework', label: 'The legal framework' },
            { href: '#authorities', label: 'Core authorities' },
            { href: '#radiology', label: 'Application to radiology' },
            { href: '#nhs-data', label: 'NHS and claims data' },
            { href: '#expert-evidence', label: 'Expert evidence' },
        ]}
        audience="Written for radiologists, lawyers, NHS legal teams and expert witnesses."
        reviewed="October 2026"
    />

    <div class="container-page space-y-12 band">
        <Figure
            label="Figure 01"
            caption="The structure of a clinical negligence claim"
            subtitle="Duty, breach, causation and damage must be considered separately."
        >
            <Diagram name="legal-questions" />
        </Figure>
    </div>

    <!-- PART I: LEGAL FRAMEWORK -->

    <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #64748b;">
        <span class="part-accent"></span>

        <div class="part-mark">
            <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
            <span class="part-mark-label">
                Part I
                <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Legal framework</span>
            </span>
        </div>

        <Section
            id="framework"
            eyebrow="Part I"
            title="The Elements of a Clinical Negligence Claim"
        >
            <p class="para">
                A poor outcome, reporting discrepancy or missed abnormality does not
                by itself establish negligence. The claimant must prove duty, breach,
                causation and compensable harm on the balance of probabilities.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Duty</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        A radiologist ordinarily owes a duty when accepting responsibility
                        for an examination, procedure or report.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Breach</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        The question is whether the radiologist exercised the reasonable
                        skill and care expected of a competent practitioner undertaking
                        that work at the time.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Causation</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        The claimant must usually prove that, but for the breach, the
                        injury or materially worse outcome would probably have been avoided.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Damage</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Negligence is not actionable without injury or loss. A reporting
                        error that causes no clinical consequence will not ordinarily
                        support an award of damages.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>
    </div>

    <!-- PART II: CORE AUTHORITIES -->

    <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #475569;">
        <span class="part-accent"></span>

        <div class="part-mark">
            <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
            <span class="part-mark-label">
                Part II
                <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Core authorities</span>
            </span>
        </div>

        <Section
            id="authorities"
            eyebrow="Part II"
            title="The Foundational Cases"
        >
            <p class="para">
                These authorities provide the essential framework for professional
                standard, hindsight, causation and consent.
            </p>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Standard of care"
            title="Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee"
            citation="[1957] 1 WLR 582"
        >
            <p class="para">
                Professional practice is not negligent where it accords with a
                responsible body of appropriately skilled medical opinion.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        The law permits a range of responsible professional practice.
                        A clinician is not negligent merely because another responsible
                        practitioner would have acted differently.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        A reasonable difference in interpretation may fall within the
                        permitted range. The question is not simply whether another
                        radiologist can identify the abnormality retrospectively.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Logical scrutiny"
            title="Bolitho v City and Hackney Health Authority"
            citation="[1998] AC 232"
        >
            <p class="para">
                Professional opinion must withstand logical analysis. A court is not
                required to accept expert evidence merely because it represents the
                opinion of a body of practitioners.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Supporting opinion must be responsible, reasoned and capable of
                        accounting for the material risks and benefits.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        An expert should explain why the report was reasonable in light
                        of the images, protocol, clinical information and practice at the
                        time. Assertion alone is insufficient.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Hindsight"
            title="Roe v Minister of Health"
            citation="[1954] 2 QB 66"
        >
            <p class="para">
                Clinical conduct is judged using the knowledge reasonably available at
                the time, not through later scientific knowledge or the eventual outcome.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        A known adverse outcome must not be allowed to redefine what was
                        reasonably foreseeable or recognisable earlier.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        The original examination must be assessed with the clinical
                        details, prior imaging and technical information available to
                        the reporter at the time.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Factual causation"
            title="Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital Management Committee"
            citation="[1969] 1 QB 428"
        >
            <p class="para">
                A breach does not establish liability unless it caused the injury.
                The conventional starting point is the “but for” test.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Ask whether the injury would probably have occurred in any event.
                        If it would, factual causation is not established.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        A missed abnormality is not enough. The claimant must usually show
                        that a competent report would have led to action that would probably
                        have prevented or reduced the harm.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Multiple causes"
            title="Wilsher v Essex Area Health Authority"
            citation="[1988] AC 1074"
        >
            <p class="para">
                Where several distinct factors could have caused the injury, proof that
                one negligent factor merely increased the risk may be insufficient.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        The claimant must ordinarily prove that the negligent act or
                        omission probably caused the injury, rather than merely being
                        one possible cause.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        This is important where outcome depended on several diseases,
                        comorbidities or treatment variables. Breach and causation must
                        remain separate.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Consent"
            title="Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board"
            citation="[2015] UKSC 11"
        >
            <p class="para">
                A clinician must take reasonable care to ensure that the patient is
                aware of material risks of the recommended treatment and any reasonable
                alternatives.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Materiality</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        A risk is material if a reasonable person in the patient's
                        position would probably attach significance to it, or if the
                        clinician should reasonably know that this patient would do so.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        The principle is particularly important in interventional
                        radiology. Consent should address material risks and reasonable
                        alternatives in a discussion tailored to the individual patient.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Figure
            label="Figure 02"
            caption="The foundational authorities"
            subtitle="Professional standard, logical scrutiny, hindsight, causation and consent."
        >
            <Diagram name="case-timeline" />
        </Figure>
    </div>

    <!-- PART III: APPLICATION TO RADIOLOGY -->

    <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #334155;">
        <span class="part-accent"></span>

        <div class="part-mark">
            <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
            <span class="part-mark-label">
                Part III
                <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Application to radiology</span>
            </span>
        </div>

        <Section
            id="radiology"
            eyebrow="Part III"
            title="Applying the Principles"
        >
            <p class="para">
                Radiological negligence is not determined simply by showing that an
                abnormality can be seen retrospectively. The reporting circumstances
                must be reconstructed.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Original study</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Review the complete source examination, including the protocol,
                        image quality, anatomical coverage and relevant prior imaging.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Clinical context</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Identify the referral information and clinical question available
                        to the original reporter. Later knowledge must not be treated as
                        contemporaneous.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Interpretation</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Distinguish a perceptual omission from an abnormality that was
                        detected but reasonably characterised differently.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Communication</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Consider whether an urgent or unexpected finding was reported,
                        escalated and acknowledged through the appropriate pathway.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Causation</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Identify what a competent report would probably have changed:
                        further imaging, referral, biopsy, treatment and eventual outcome.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>
    </div>

    <!-- PART IV: NHS AND CLAIMS DATA -->

    <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #1e293b;">
        <span class="part-accent"></span>

        <div class="part-mark">
            <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
            <span class="part-mark-label">
                Part IV
                <span class="part-mark-label-sub">NHS and claims data</span>
            </span>
        </div>

        <Section
            id="nhs-data"
            eyebrow="Part IV"
            title="NHS Claims Context"
        >
            <p class="para">
                NHS Resolution publishes national data on clinical negligence claims,
                expenditure and methods of resolution. These figures describe the claims
                system; they do not measure the incidence of clinical negligence.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">National position</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        In 2025/26, NHS Resolution received 15,236 new clinical negligence
                        claims and reported incidents. Eighty-four per cent of claims were
                        resolved without formal legal proceedings.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Interpreting the data</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        Claim notifications, settled claims, payments and financial
                        provisions are different measures. A claim is not equivalent to
                        a judicial finding of negligence.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="para-sm">
                Source: NHS Resolution, <em>Annual Report and Accounts 2025/26</em>.
            </p>
        </Section>

        <Section
            eyebrow="Radiology evidence"
            title="Incidental Findings in Abdominal Radiology Claims"
        >
            <p class="para">
                A retrospective review by five abdominal-radiology expert witnesses
                examined 112 medicolegal cases involving 176 examinations.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Findings</h3>
                    <ul class="list">
                        <li>Forty-one of 112 allegations, or 37%, concerned incidental pathology.</li>
                        <li>Tumour was the most frequent incidental-finding allegation.</li>
                        <li>Twelve of the 41 incidental interpretations were judged substandard.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Limitation</h3>
                    <p class="para-sm">
                        This was a selected expert-witness series, not a national dataset.
                        It does not show that 37% of all NHS radiology claims concern
                        incidental findings.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="para-sm">
                Source: Halligan S, Blunt D, Boone D, Higginson T and Plumb AA,
                <em>Clinical Radiology</em>, 2026;92:107162.
            </p>
        </Section>
    </div>

    <!-- PART V: EXPERT EVIDENCE -->

    <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #0f172a;">
        <span class="part-accent"></span>

        <div class="part-mark">
            <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
            <span class="part-mark-label">
                Part V
                <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Expert evidence</span>
            </span>
        </div>

        <Section
            id="expert-evidence"
            eyebrow="Part V"
            title="A Defensible Expert Approach"
        >
            <p class="para">
                The expert assists the court rather than either party. Opinion should
                distinguish what is visible retrospectively, what should reasonably have
                been identified prospectively and what difference a competent report
                would probably have made.
            </p>

            <div class="entry-list">
                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Breach</h3>
                    <ul class="list">
                        <li>Review the original source images where available.</li>
                        <li>Record the protocol, quality and relevant limitations.</li>
                        <li>Identify the information available to the original reporter.</li>
                        <li>Review prior studies in their proper chronology.</li>
                        <li>Explain why the report did or did not fall outside responsible practice.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <h3 class="entry-title">Causation</h3>
                    <ul class="list">
                        <li>State what the competent report should have said.</li>
                        <li>Identify the probable clinical response.</li>
                        <li>Separate radiological opinion from other specialist evidence.</li>
                        <li>Avoid assuming that earlier diagnosis necessarily meant a better outcome.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Section>

        <Callout tone="caution" title="Scope">
            <p>
                This page provides an educational summary of foundational principles.
                Detailed legislation, professional standards, recent decisions, candour
                and AI-assisted reporting are addressed separately. It is not legal advice
                or a substitute for the primary authorities.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </div>
</BaseLayout>